Z||000000||Banzai||12-03-2001||06:59 AM||||well after over 300 hours of work I took her for a drive yesterday to check things out. drove about four miles stopped and got out and the right front rotor was glowing red and smokin like a bit--.  Is this a bad caliper???  ALL brake parts are new----Hubs, bearings, rotors, pads, calipers, bolts, sleeves, stainless lines, banjo bolts etc. Did I get a bunk caliper?????  Also are the rear backer plates specific to each side??? I couldn't find any markers on them like the front and the left rear is rubbing on the rotor.  Help me----------------<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||11
Z||000001||PBJ||12-03-2001||08:47 AM||||sounds like you have either a bad caliper or flex line, something is not allowing brake to release on that wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Banzai||12-03-2001||08:21 PM||||Well, I exchanged the caliper, rotor and pads under warranty today. No hassles. I've got the new rotor on and the caliper painted. Do the calipers have an internal valve?? Is there a compensating circut in the master cylinder that could be clogged?? <BR>Any ideas on the rear rubbing???? <BR>Banzai !!!!!!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Banzai (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||scarydok||12-05-2001||12:32 AM||||Replace the Brake Hose!  The hose that comes from the steel line and attaches to the caliper.  I have had that go bad and had glowing rotors.  That should take care of your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SKIDMARK||12-05-2001||01:01 AM||||I agree with the brake hose or maybe you got a bad caliper. I've seen both on my Fiero. Got a fresh rebuilt caliper from the local parts store and dumped a thimble full of sand out of it. Seems they forgot to take it apart to rebuild it. Just sand-blasted, new seal and back on the shelf. I talked the guy into giving me my cores back along with free rebuild kits and did it myself.<BR>I've never heard of having something plugged up in the master cylinder or proportioning valve. Although it's possible, It's not likely. Check the hose, it's pretty common. <BR>The backing plate (splash guard)is probably just bent a little. There isn't much clearance between the rotor and splash guard. Just bend it with your hand to make more clearance.<p>[This message has been edited by SKIDMARK (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Banzai||12-05-2001||06:51 AM||||Thanks all.  The car has new stainless flex lines.  I put the new caliper and pads on. It doesn't seem to be sticking but niether did the other one.<P>AHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH, another problem.  I was checking the rears after going back through the fronts. I had a fluid leak at the right rear caliper, where the flexline goes into the caliper.  I replaced the washers and it still leaked. I took a closer look and found that the sealing area is so pitted that there is no way it would seal right.  So I got out the file and fixed that--------- I went to TORQUE(30 ft. lbs. per Chilton) the banjo bolt for the third time and the threads stripped out of the caliper. <P>I Hope they cover this one too.<P>Oh how I wish I could get some seat time in before the salt trucks come.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Banzai||12-06-2001||06:58 AM||||Well, I took the rear caliper to Murry's and they exchanged it-- no problem.  I installed it and all seems to be Ok in the back.<P>Due to a lack of work my employer is sending me to school and I took an autoshop class just to get some more hands on .<P>I took the car in to class yesterday(big brownie points from the teacher). We put the car on the hoist and found that the left front caliper is dragging a little bit(remember these are all new(rebuilt) parts.<BR>He came to the conclusion that it was another bad caliper. #3 out of four. When will it end.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
